      "thinking": "The real issue is that collinear features make the model ill-conditioned, so TF32 rounding wipes out the subtle differences. The fix is to combine the collinear weights analytically first\u2014computing an effective weight in full precision before doing a single multiply\u2014so the result stays consistent regardless of TF32. I need to be careful not to change anything else in the process though.\n\nWorking through the numbers: the effective weight comes out to roughly 0.05 per pound, with bias giving a threshold around 86 pounds, matching expected behavior. I'm defining an EFFECTIVE_WEIGHT constant that folds the two collinear weights together, then writing fraud_logit to apply TF32 rounding (if applicable) to just that single weight and the charge before multiplying\u2014accepting that rounding the small effective weight still introduces a tiny relative error, but one small enough not to shift the decision boundary meaningfully.\n\nI want to keep rounding on both operands for faithfulness even though the constant is precomputed in full precision on the host, since that's closer to what actually happens in the matmul. Now I should check other callers and the test file to make sure this holds up.\n\n",
